      "largest_q_z": 0.351653199107999,
      "scope": "analytic implication conditional on nonvanishing parity amplitude and a nonzero-measure smooth sublevel interval; numerical branch data establish the premise only as scout evidence here",
      "statement": "If along q->infinity the smooth radii converge and |z| stays bounded below, the first odd/cross-fiber shell has O(1) distance while O(q) even offsets sample a nonzero interval of the smooth orbit below that threshold. Hence single-threshold RG1 has degree Theta(q), not bounded degree."
    },
    "phase_type_to_cell_blowup_no_go": {
      "consequence": "The phase-orbit graph is an internal/state-space geometry. It cannot by itself source sparse spatial adjacency among arbitrarily many indistinguishable cells.",
      "statement": "If an M-cell adjacency rule depends only on internal phase types a_x and a_y, then the cell graph is a blow-up of the finite phase-type graph. For a cell of type a its degree is sum_b B_ab n_b (minus the diagonal correction). Therefore any neighboring type with occupancy n_b=Theta(M) produces degree Theta(M). A bounded-degree physical cell graph for arbitrary macroscopic occupancies requires pair-specific relational data beyond phase type, or an additional matching/selection law."
    },
    "smooth_rank6_cycle_theorem": {
      "scope": "conditional on a nonzero localized branch with all three smooth modal radii positive; no claim that this family is selected by strict FIN",
      "statement": "For active modes 3,4,5 with positive radii and gcd(3,4,5)=1, the translation-orbit map t -> (rho3 e^{i3t},rho4 e^{i4t},rho5 e^{i5t}) is an injective smooth immersion of S1. Therefore for sufficiently fine uniform Z_q sampling, the nearest relational states are the adjacent samples +/-1 and RG1 returns C_q."
    }
